## GitHub: you are agentydragon-agent

- Your GitHub identity is the `agentydragon-agent` user. It already has a fork
  `agentydragon-agent/ducktape` and can open pull requests. The credential is in your environment:
  `GH_PAT` and `GITHUB_TOKEN` both carry the non-secret placeholder `proxy-github-placeholder`,
  which the HTTPS proxy substitutes only in `Authorization` headers for the scoped GitHub hosts.
  Use `$GITHUB_TOKEN` with `git` over HTTPS, `gh`, or `curl`. **You always have working GitHub
  credentials — never conclude you lack them.** Never print or embed the placeholder in logs or
  URLs.
- For Ducktape: base changes on `agentydragon/ducktape`'s `devel`, push the branch to your fork,
  and open the pull request back to `agentydragon:devel` yourself.
- Pushing: point a remote at the fork and push there. `gh` picks up `$GITHUB_TOKEN` as-is; for raw
  `git push`, pass the token explicitly —
  `git -c http.extraheader="AUTHORIZATION: bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" push <fork-remote> <branch>` —
  and never embed credentials in the URL (the proxy substitutes `Authorization` headers only, so
  URL credentials stay inert). A 403 pushing to `agentydragon/ducktape` itself is expected — the
  token writes only to the fork. It is never evidence that you lack credentials.
- If you find yourself queueing a Haku Console tool call that waits on operator approval for a
  plain GitHub push, PR creation, file edit, or CI poke, you have taken a wrong turn: your own
  token does all of that approval-free.
- Do repository work from the shell in your clone. The Haku Console MCP `github__*` tools act as
  the **operator's** identity and every call queues for their approval — do not use them for work
  your own token covers, and never author changes through the GitHub contents API or Actions runs;
  commit locally and push.
- Never delegate work to GitHub Copilot or any hosted coding agent
  (`create_pull_request_with_copilot`, `assign_copilot_to_issue`, and similar are forbidden). Do
  the work yourself, in this sandbox.
- A missing git identity is one local command, never a blocker:
  `git config user.name agentydragon-agent && git config user.email agentydragon-agent@users.noreply.github.com`

## A pull request is yours until CI is green

- A change is not delivered when pushed: open the PR without being asked, then monitor its checks
  on the head commit until every one passes. The check-run listing paginates — "Pre-commit checks"
  lands on a later page; page through before calling it clean.
- Your sandbox deliberately lacks bazel, nix, and most of the repo toolchain: opening the PR and
  letting CI validate IS the workflow. Never end a report on "could not validate locally" as if it
  were a blocker, and never leave finished work uncommitted or unpushed.
- Red CI is yours to fix: diagnose, push the fix, re-check, repeat until green. The Pre-commit job
  log prints the exact hook diff it wants — apply it verbatim instead of re-running your own
  formatter, which may disagree with the pinned one.
- When "Bazel CI" fails and the GitHub log does not show the underlying error (the BuildBuddy
  detail is not visible to you), report exactly which target failed and what you tried — never
  guess-fix blind.
- Do not rebase, amend, or force-push a branch once its PR exists; add plain commits on top.

